---
/*
 * Webpage for the JURIX 2026 spotlight tutorial "Rules as Executed: When
 * machine-executable laws are published". JURIX links to this page from its
 * workshops-and-tutorials overview, so the address has to stay put.
 *
 * Unlike the paper next to it, this page is not frozen: the time slot and the
 * room are added here as they become known. There is no slide deck to publish:
 * the tutorial is presented from the demo. The program below
 * follows the accepted proposal; the paper's own metadata (title, DOI) is read
 * from the generated meta JSON so the reference cannot drift from the paper.
 */
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Base from '~/layouts/Base.astro';
import DocsOutline from '~/components/DocsOutline.astro';
import paper from '~/research/rules-as-executed.meta.json';
import portalScreenshot from '~/assets/portaal-en-screenshot.png';

const pathname = '/research/jurix-2026-tutorial';
const tutorialTitle = 'Rules as Executed';
const tutorialSubtitle = 'When machine-executable laws are published';
const title = `${tutorialTitle}: JURIX 2026 tutorial · RegelRecht`;
const description =
  'Spotlight tutorial at JURIX 2026 in Toulouse, 8 December 2026: law ' +
  'execution published as a machine-executable specification, run live.';

const demoUrl = 'https://demo.regelrecht.rijks.app';
const jurixUrl = 'https://www.irit.fr/jurix2026/workshops-and-tutorials/';
const repoUrl = 'https://github.com/MinBZK/regelrecht';
const paperHref = '/research/rules-as-executed';
const paperDoiUrl = `https://doi.org/${paper.doi}`;
const contactEmail = 'anne.schuth@rijksoverheid.nl';

// The sidebar outline: the h2s of the page, in order. Kept by hand next to the
// headings below, because the page is authored here and not rendered from
// markdown.
const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: 'about', text: 'Run the rule yourself' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'program', text: 'Program' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'audience', text: 'Who it is for' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'materials', text: 'Materials' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'organizers', text: 'Organizers' },
];
---

<Base
  lang="en"
  kind="docs"
  pathname={pathname}
  title={title}
  description={description}
>
  <nldd-sidebar-section
    no-collapse
    sidebar-label="On this page"
    sm-padding-bottom="32"
    md-padding-bottom="64"
    lg-padding-bottom="96"
  >
    <nldd-title slot="header" size="1">
      <h1>{tutorialTitle}</h1>
      <p slot="supporting-text">{tutorialSubtitle}</p>
    </nldd-title>
    <nldd-container slot="sidebar" padding="16">
      <nldd-button-group orientation="vertical">
        <nldd-button
          href={demoUrl}
          text="Open the demo"
          end-icon="external-link"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener"
          appearance="secondary"></nldd-button>
        <nldd-button
          href={jurixUrl}
          text="JURIX 2026 tutorials"
          end-icon="external-link"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener"
          appearance="secondary"></nldd-button>
      </nldd-button-group>
      <nldd-spacer size="12"></nldd-spacer>
      <DocsOutline headings={headings} />
    </nldd-container>
    <nldd-rich-text centered>
      <p>
        <strong>Anne Schuth and Eelco Hotting</strong><br />
        <em>Dutch Digital Service, Ministry of Economic Affairs and Climate</em
        ><br />
        Spotlight tutorial at{' '}
        <a href={jurixUrl}>JURIX 2026</a>, Toulouse<br />
        8 December 2026, 90 minutes. Time and room follow once the program is
        out.
      </p>

      <h2 id="about">Run the rule yourself</h2>
      <p>
        Somewhere in a government system, a rule decides whether you get a
        benefit, a permit or a fine. The statute behind it is published. The
        rule is not: it lives in databases, spreadsheets and bespoke software.
        No branch of government sees it whole. Parliament cannot see what its
        laws do in operation, and a court reviews decisions whose logic it
        cannot inspect.
      </p>
      <p>
        Our <a href={paperHref}>position paper</a> proposes to publish that rule
        as a machine-executable specification: an interpretation of the
        statute, article by article, in a format built to be read by legal
        professionals. The organization decides cases with the specification it
        publishes, so whoever receives a decision can run the rule again and
        check the outcome.
      </p>
      <p>
        RegelRecht is the reference implementation. In ninety minutes we open
        real Dutch statutes next to their specification, change a threshold
        and watch who loses an allowance. Then we follow one application from
        the citizen's screen to the case worker's desk.
      </p>
      <p>
        Open the <a href={demoUrl}>demo</a> on your own laptop and try to break
        it. We expect to be argued with, and we have left room for it.
      </p>
    </nldd-rich-text>
    <nldd-spacer size="24"></nldd-spacer>
    {/*
     * The English portal screenshot the landing page shows, processed by Astro's
     * <Image> and slotted into nldd-image. It sits between two rich-texts
     * because nldd-rich-text lays out prose, not media.
     */}
    <nldd-image>
      <Image
        src={portalScreenshot}
        alt="Screenshot of the demo: a citizen's overview of allowances and benefits, each amount computed from the published specification."
        widths={[600, 900, 1200, 1800]}
        sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 900px, 100vw"
        format="webp"
        loading="lazy"
      />
    </nldd-image>
    <nldd-spacer size="24"></nldd-spacer>
    <nldd-rich-text centered>
      <h2 id="program">Program</h2>
      <p>
        We follow the tabs of the demo and take the discussion as it comes.
        Timings are indicative: where the room wants to stop is where we stop.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The problem</strong> (10 min). Why the rule as executed must be
          independently verifiable, and why today no branch of government sees
          it whole.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Laws</strong> (20 min). A statute and its specification, article
          by article. Then the graph: an allowance that depends on
          being insured, which depends on whether you are detained.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Scenarios</strong> (10 min). "Someone under 18 gets no
          allowance", written as a sentence and run as a test. Change the law
          and see which sentences stop being true.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Simulation</strong> (5 min). A generated population through
          the same laws. Turn a threshold and count who falls out, before the
          amendment is passed.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Citizen and entrepreneur</strong> (10 min). Merijn, single
          parent and self-employed, sees what he is entitled to, each figure
          traceable to its source, and is asked only what no register knows. Claudia wants a terrace in front of her
          coffee shop and meets three authorities in one view.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Case system</strong> (10 min). The other side of the counter:
          the engine recomputes what the citizen saw. Where the law gives the
          power, the case worker departs from the outcome, and the ground is
          recorded.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The same article, four ways</strong> (15 min). One article in
          our YAML, in Catala, as an OpenFisca-style formula and as
          LegalRuleML. Come and tell us why we should have used yours.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Closing</strong> (10 min). What is built, what is not, and
          the questions we cannot answer without this room.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="audience">Who it is for</h2>
      <p>
        For people who formalize law, and for people who worry about what that
        does to it. There are no prerequisites and there is no programming.
        Bring a laptop or phone, and your objections.
      </p>

      <h2 id="materials">Materials</h2>
      <p>
        We present from the demo itself, slides included, so everything shown
        in the room is already open to you. To prepare, read the introduction
        and Section 4 of the{' '}
        <a href={paperHref}>position paper</a>{' '}
        (<a href={paperDoiUrl}>doi:{paper.doi}</a>).
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href={demoUrl}>The demo</a>, nothing to install</li>
        <li><a href="/docs/">The documentation</a></li>
        <li><a href="/rfcs/">The design RFCs</a></li>
        <li><a href={repoUrl}>The source code</a>, under EUPL-1.2</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="organizers">Organizers</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Anne Schuth</strong> is the technical lead of RegelRecht at the
        Dutch Digital Service. He holds a PhD in information retrieval and
        worked at Google, Microsoft Research and Spotify.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Eelco Hotting</strong> is a strategic adviser at the Dutch
        Digital Service and co-author of the position paper.
      </p>
      <p>
        Questions about the tutorial:{' '}
        <a href={`mailto:${contactEmail}`}>{contactEmail}</a>.
      </p>
    </nldd-rich-text>
  </nldd-sidebar-section>
</Base>
